==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-holler.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈhɑ.ləɹ/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈhɒ.lə/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒlə(r)}}

===Etymology 1===
American variant of [[holla]], [[hallo]] or [[hollo]]. According to [[w:Daniel Cassidy|Daniel Cassidy]], the term is derived from the Irish Gaelic ''oll-bhúir'', pronounced ''h-oll-oor'', meaning a terrific yell, a great roar. <ref>Cassidy, D: "How the Irish invented Slang", page 179, CounterPunch Press, 2007, {{ISBN|978-1-904859-60-4}}</ref> However, the pronounciation of the Irish term as claimed by Cassidy turned out to be wrong.<ref>[https://cassidyslangscam.wordpress.com/tag/ollbhuir/  Cassidese Glossary – Holler ], cassidyslangscam.wordpress.com</ref> Also, the Irish term is not included in the first edition of [[w:Patrick S. Dinneen|Patrick S. Dinneen]]'s Irish-English Dictionary (Foclóir Gaedhilge agus Béarla), published in 1904.<ref>[https://celt.ucc.ie//Dinneen1.pdf#page=496 Page 496 - oll] in [https://celt.ucc.ie//Dinneen1.pdf  Foclóir Gaedhilge agus Béarla - An Irish-English Dictionary], celt.ucc.ie</ref> 

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[yell]], [[shout]].
#: ''I heard a '''holler''' from over the fence.''
# By extension, any communication to get somebody's attention.
#: ''If you need anything, just give me a '''holler'''.''

